---

<BaseLayout
  title="Pest Control Requirements in State Jail Standards: A 30-State Comparison"
  description="The pest and vermin provisions of the jail standards of 30 states, from California, Florida, New York and Texas to Utah and Virginia, quoted from each primary source, with frequency, provider, and legal force side by side."
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'State Jail Standards', url: `${SITE_URL}/state-jail-standards/` }]}
>
  <style>
    .compare { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 0.93rem; margin: 1.25rem 0 2rem; }
    .compare th, .compare td { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-rule); padding: 0.7rem 0.6rem; text-align: left; vertical-align: top; }
    .compare th { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-weight: 600; background: var(--color-surface, transparent); }
    .compare td.state a { font-family: var(--font-serif); font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-primary); }
    .compare blockquote { margin: 0; padding: 0; border: 0; font-style: normal; }
    .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; width: min(1180px, calc(100vw - 32px)); position: relative; left: 50%; transform: translateX(-50%); }
    .compare col.c-state { width: 10%; } .compare col.c-inst { width: 18%; } .compare col.c-force { width: 19%; }
    .compare col.c-quote { width: 31%; } .compare col.c-freq { width: 22%; }
    .compare blockquote { font-size: 0.92rem; line-height: 1.45; }
    .note { color: var(--color-text-muted); font-size: 0.93rem; }
    @media (max-width: 760px) {
      .compare thead { display: none; }
      .compare, .compare tbody, .compare tr, .compare td { display: block; width: 100%; }
      .compare tr { border-bottom: 2px solid var(--color-rule); padding: 0.6rem 0; }
      .compare td { border: 0; padding: 0.3rem 0; }
      .compare td::before { content: attr(data-label); display: block; font-weight: 600; font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--color-text-meta); }
    }
  </style>

  <h1>Pest Control Requirements in State Jail Standards: A 30-State Comparison</h1>

  <p>
    What a state's jail standards say about pests, side by side. Each row quotes the provision from its primary
    source and links to the authority page, which gives the full text, who it applies to, the records that show it
    was met, and the date the text was verified.
  </p>

  <p class="note">
    Legal force is stated as each source states it. State rules and statutes bind; association standards are
    professional standards tied to inspection or accreditation. Verify against the primary source before relying on
    any row for a legal purpose.
  </p>


  <p class="note">
    Jump to a state:
    {rows.map((r, i) => (<>{i > 0 && ' · '}<a href={`/authorities/${r.slug}/`}>{r.state}</a></>))}
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="compare">
      <colgroup><col class="c-state" /><col class="c-inst" /><col class="c-force" /><col class="c-quote" /><col class="c-freq" /></colgroup>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">State</th>
          <th scope="col">Instrument</th>
          <th scope="col">Legal force (as the source states it)</th>
          <th scope="col">Pest provision (verbatim)</th>
          <th scope="col">Frequency and provider named</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {rows.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td class="state" data-label="State"><a href={`/authorities/${r.slug}/`}>{r.state}</a></td>
            <td data-label="Instrument">{r.instrument}</td>
            <td data-label="Legal force">{r.force}</td>
            <td data-label="Pest provision (verbatim)"><blockquote>“{r.quote}”</blockquote></td>
            <td data-label="Frequency and provider">{r.frequency}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What the Comparison Shows</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A fixed frequency and a licensed provider together in binding state text: Virginia and New Jersey.</strong>
      Virginia requires service at least quarterly by a licensed pest control business or personnel certified by its
      agriculture department. New Jersey requires licensed pest control professionals at least once a month. New
      Mexico's association accreditation standard does the same, with monthly inspection by a licensed exterminator.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A licensed or certified provider, with the schedule left to the facility.</strong> Oklahoma (licensed
      professionals under contract, on a schedule set in facility policy), Indiana (licensed extermination as often as
      necessary, after a weekly inspection for evidence of insects and rodents), Florida (commercial licensed companies
      where the jail has no certified operators), Massachusetts (a person with appropriate licensing, when pests exist)
      and Iowa (anyone spraying must be state-certified).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A fixed frequency without a licensed provider.</strong> Maryland (quarterly services; the licensed
      exterminator appears in the Commission's audit worksheet, not the regulation), Pennsylvania (monthly, by a
      qualified person), Michigan (monthly inspections by a trained designee), Ohio (monthly inspections by trained
      staff) and Idaho (monthly inspections, an association standard). Colorado puts quarterly inspection by a pest
      control business in a best-practice note.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The most common form is a plan, policy, or program with no frequency.</strong> Colorado, Illinois,
      Kentucky, Minnesota, New York, North Carolina, Oregon, South Carolina, Tennessee, Washington and Wisconsin.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Outcome standards.</strong> Montana states the outcome in one sentence. Iowa requires a pest-free jail.
      Florida requires facilities kept free of all insects and rodents. Nevada requires effective measures to
      eliminate vermin, then specifies exclusion.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No facility pest-control requirement at all: Texas, California and Arkansas.</strong> Texas's vermin
      rules are construction rules. California's vermin rule is about infested people, and its kitchens must meet the
      Retail Food Code. Arkansas checks detainees for lice or other pests at intake. In these states the jail's own
      plan and contract are the whole record.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Integrated pest management by name: New Jersey only.</strong> Its rule requires IPM practices to control
      the use of toxic pesticides.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Occupant protection.</strong> Tennessee and Utah remove inmates from treatment areas when there is a
      risk of illness. Indiana removes them when spraying or fogging cannot properly be done with them present. Iowa
      bars direct exposure of prisoners and staff.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Exclusion to the mesh.</strong> Sixteen-mesh screening is written into the rules of Illinois,
      Massachusetts, Nevada, North Carolina and South Carolina.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Records and correction.</strong> Pennsylvania requires documentation of every pest or vermin treatment
      applied. Wisconsin and Illinois require the program itself to be documented. New Mexico's process indicators ask
      for documentation that identified deficiencies were corrected, and Utah's weekly inspection policy must contain a
      process for reporting and tracking maintenance issues.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    A policy is not a closed complaint, and a grievance or claim turns on the second. The
    <a href="/tools/grievance-loop/">Grievance-to-Work-Order Loop</a> and
    <a href="/tools/vermin-grievance-file/">The Vermin Grievance File</a> are free tools for closing it, and the
    <a href="/tools/compliance-map/">Pest Compliance Map</a> pairs each requirement with the record that shows it.
  </p>

  <h2>Coverage</h2>

  <p>
    Thirty states as of September 2026. The other twenty are being added as each primary source is verified; several
    of them run unified state systems with no county jails. Federal detention standards (ICE, U.S. Marshals Service,
    Bureau of Prisons), the FDA Food Code, and OSHA are on the <a href="/authorities/">authority index</a>. Association
    standards that are copyrighted are recorded by identifier only. To suggest a state or send a correction, write to
    <a href="mailto:trent@frazerappliedentomology.com">trent@frazerappliedentomology.com</a>.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
